#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:9
msgid "Configuring environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:11
msgid ""
"**Goal:** This tutorial will show you how to prepare your ROS 2 environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:13
msgid "**Tutorial level:** Beginner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:15
msgid "**Time:** 5 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:19
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:22
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:24
msgid ""
"ROS 2 relies on the notion of combining workspaces using the shell "
"environment. \"Workspace\" is a ROS term for the location on your system "
"where you're developing with ROS 2. In a typical ROS 2 setup, the core ROS 2 "
"installation is the underlay. A local workspace sourced after that "
"installation is an overlay because it is layered on top of the underlay. The "
"same workspace can act as an underlay for another workspace that is sourced "
"later. When developing with ROS 2, you will typically have several "
"workspaces active concurrently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:31
msgid ""
"Combining workspaces makes developing against different versions of ROS 2, "
"or against different sets of packages, easier. It also allows the "
"installation of several ROS 2 distributions (or \"distros\", e.g. Dashing "
"and Eloquent) on the same computer and switching between them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:34
msgid ""
"This is accomplished by sourcing setup files every time you open a new "
"shell, or by adding the source command to your shell startup script once. "
"Without sourcing the setup files, you won't be able to access ROS 2 "
"commands, or find or use ROS 2 packages. In other words, you won't be able "
"to use ROS 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:39
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:41
msgid ""
"Before starting these tutorials, install ROS 2 by following the instructions "
"on the ROS 2 :doc:`Installation` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:43
msgid ""
"The commands used in this tutorial assume you followed the binary packages "
"installation guide for your operating system (deb packages for Linux). You "
"can still follow along if you built from source, but the path to your setup "
"files will likely be different. You also won't be able to use the ``sudo apt "
"install ros-<distro>-<package>`` command (used frequently in the beginner "
"level tutorials) if you install from source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:47
msgid ""
"If you are using Linux or macOS, but are not already familiar with the "
"shell, `this tutorial <https://www.linux.com/training-tutorials/bash-101-"
"working-cli/>`__ will help."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:50
msgid "Tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:53
msgid "1 Source the setup files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:55
msgid ""
"You will need to run this command on every new shell you open to have access "
"to the ROS 2 commands, like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:59
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:92
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:135
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:173
msgid "Linux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:61
msgid "$ source /opt/ros/rolling/setup.bash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:66
msgid ""
"Replace ``.bash`` with your shell if you're not using bash. Possible values "
"are: ``setup.bash``, ``setup.sh``, ``setup.zsh``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:69
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:100
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:141
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:185
msgid "macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:71
msgid "$ . ~/ros2_install/ros2-osx/setup.bash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:75
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:108
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:147
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:197
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:77
msgid "$ call C:\\dev\\ros2\\local_setup.bat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:82
msgid ""
"The exact command depends on where you installed ROS 2. If you're having "
"problems, ensure the file path leads to your installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:86
msgid "2 Add sourcing to your shell startup script"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:88
msgid ""
"If you don't want to have to source the setup file every time you open a new "
"shell (skipping task 1), then you can add the command to your shell startup "
"script:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:94
msgid "$ echo \"source /opt/ros/rolling/setup.bash\" >> ~/.bashrc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:98
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:106
msgid ""
"To undo this, locate your system's shell startup script and remove the "
"appended source command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:102
msgid "$ echo \"source ~/ros2_install/ros2-osx/setup.bash\" >> ~/.bash_profile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:110
msgid ""
"Only for PowerShell users, create a folder in 'My Documents' called "
"'WindowsPowerShell'. Within 'WindowsPowerShell', create file 'Microsoft."
"PowerShell_profile.ps1'. Inside the file, paste:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:114
msgid "$ C:\\dev\\ros2_rolling\\local_setup.ps1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:118
msgid ""
"PowerShell will request permission to run this script every time a new shell "
"is opened. To avoid that issue you can run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:121
msgid "$ Unblock-File C:\\dev\\ros2_rolling\\local_setup.ps1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:125
msgid "To undo this, remove the new 'Microsoft.PowerShell_profile.ps1' file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:128
msgid "3 Check environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:130
msgid ""
"Sourcing ROS 2 setup files will set several environment variables necessary "
"for operating ROS 2. If you ever have problems finding or using your ROS 2 "
"packages, make sure that your environment is properly set up using the "
"following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:137
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:143
msgid "$ printenv | grep -i ROS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:149
msgid "$ set | findstr -i ROS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:153
msgid "Check that variables like ``ROS_DISTRO`` and ``ROS_VERSION`` are set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:157
msgid ""
"ROS_VERSION=2\n"
"ROS_PYTHON_VERSION=3\n"
"ROS_DISTRO=rolling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:161
msgid ""
"If the environment variables are not set correctly, return to the ROS 2 "
"package installation section of the installation guide you followed. If you "
"need more specific help (because environment setup files can come from "
"different places), you can `get answers <https://robotics.stackexchange.com/"
">`__ from the community."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:165
msgid "3.1 The ``ROS_DOMAIN_ID`` variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:167
msgid ""
"See the `domain ID </ROS-Framework/nodes/About-Domain-ID>` article for "
"details on ROS domain IDs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:169
msgid ""
"Once you have determined a unique integer for your group of ROS 2 nodes, you "
"can set the environment variable with the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:175
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:187
msgid "$ export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=<your_domain_id>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:179
#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:191
msgid ""
"To maintain this setting between shell sessions, you can add the command to "
"your shell startup script:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:181
msgid "$ echo \"export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=<your_domain_id>\" >> ~/.bashrc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:193
msgid "$ echo \"export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=<your_domain_id>\" >> ~/.bash_profile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:199
msgid "$ set ROS_DOMAIN_ID=<your_domain_id>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:203
msgid "If you want to make this permanent between shell sessions, also run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:205
msgid "$ setx ROS_DOMAIN_ID <your_domain_id>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:210
msgid "3.2 The ``ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE`` variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:212
msgid ""
"By default, ROS 2 communication is not limited to localhost. "
"``ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE`` environment variable allows you to limit "
"ROS 2 discovery range. Using ``ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE`` is helpful in "
"certain settings, such as classrooms, where multiple robots may publish to "
"the same topic causing strange behaviors. See :ref:`Improved Dynamic "
"Discovery <ImprovedDynamicDiscovery>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:218
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:220
msgid ""
"The ROS 2 development environment needs to be correctly configured before "
"use. This can be done in two ways: either sourcing the setup files in every "
"new shell you open, or adding the source command to your startup script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:223
msgid ""
"If you ever face any problems locating or using packages with ROS 2, the "
"first thing you should do is check your environment variables and ensure "
"they are set to the version and distro you intended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:226
msgid "Next steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment.rst:228
msgid ""
"Now that you have a working ROS 2 installation and you know how to source "
"its setup files, you can start learning the ins and outs of ROS 2 with the :"
"doc:`turtlesim tool <Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim>`."
msgstr ""
